      {
        "id": "p9-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$$\\frac{1}{x}>1$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$0<x<1$",
          "worked": "Restriction: $x\\ne0$. Multiplying by $x^2>0$ on this domain:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&>x^2\\\\x^2-x&<0\\\\x(x-1)&<0\\end{aligned}$$\nThe upward-opening quadratic has zeroes $0$ and $1$, so it is negative between them. Hence $0<x<1$, which satisfies the restriction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$$\\frac{3}{x}<1$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x<0$ or $x>3$",
          "worked": "Restriction: $x\\ne0$. Multiplying by $x^2>0$ on this domain:\n$$\\begin{aligned}3x&<x^2\\\\x^2-3x&>0\\\\x(x-3)&>0\\end{aligned}$$\nThe upward-opening quadratic has zeroes $0$ and $3$, so it is positive outside these zeroes. Hence $x<0$ or $x>3$, which satisfies the restriction.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 39
      },
      {
        "id": "p9-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$$4+\\frac{3}{x}\\geq0$$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x\\leq-\\frac34$ or $x>0$",
          "worked": "Restriction: $x\\ne0$. Multiplying by $x^2>0$ on this domain:\n$$\\begin{aligned}4x^2+3x&\\geq0\\\\x(4x+3)&\\geq0\\end{aligned}$$\nThe upward-opening quadratic has zeroes $-\\frac34$ and $0$, so it is non-negative when $x\\leq-\\frac34$ or $x\\geq0$. Excluding $x=0$ gives $x\\leq-\\frac34$ or $x>0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 43
      }
    ]
